course: dictionary
lesson: 5520
title: Learn: coming to know something new
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To learn is to come to know something new, or to get a new skill. Learning to swim, learning to read and learning from mistakes are all kinds of learning. «Today we learn to divide» means that by the end of the lesson you will know how to divide.
explain: Learn can also mean to remember something word for word. To learn something by heart means you can say it without looking. Learn about something means to find out about it: «we learned about volcanoes» means we found out facts about volcanoes.
explain: Learn has four forms: learn, learns, learned or learnt, and learning. Learned and learnt both mean the same, so either one is correct. Choose the form that fits the sentence: «He has learnt to swim» or «She learns quickly».
fact: Learn means to come to know something new, or to get a new skill.
fact: Learn can mean to remember something word for word, as in learn a poem by heart.
fact: Learn about something means to find out about it.
fact: «Learned» and «learnt» are both correct past forms of learn.
fact: «Portly hasn't learnt to swim very well yet» means he is still getting better at swimming.
exercise: «Today we learn to divide.» What does learn mean here? | To forget something old | To buy a new book | To shout loudly | *To come to know how to do something new
exercise: Jo wants to learn a poem by heart. What does learn by heart mean? | To throw the poem away | To read it once and then forget it | To draw a picture of it | *To remember it word for word
exercise: «Portly hasn't learnt to swim very well yet.» What does this tell you? | He has never been near water | He has finished learning to swim | He swims faster than everyone else | *He is still getting better at swimming
test: «She ___ about volcanoes last week.» Which word fits the gap? | learns | learn | learning | *learnt
test: «Gulls are clever: they have learned that people carry food.» What does learned mean here? | Lost track of | Bought for | Forgot about | *Found out, came to know
exam: «Reading slowly is not the same as being stupid; many clever people learn differently.» What does this sentence suggest? | Only stupid people learn differently | *Clever people can learn in different ways | Everyone learns in exactly the same way | Clever people never read slowly
